Broken Access Control on Firmware Management (Downgrade & DoS Risk)
Severity: High
Affected Component: System Tab (Firmware Management)

Description: The web application allows a low-privileged Info-User to view, select, and potentially schedule or activate older firmware files. This represents a severe lack of authorization controls on a critical system function.

Observation: While authenticated as the Info-User, navigating to System -> Firmware Management revealed that the user could not only view the firmware history but could actively select older, outdated firmware files (fw_7.08.002 and fw_7.08.025) for activation. The Start Update button appeared grayed out, but the ability to schedule these files indicates a severe backend authorization flaw. (Note: The older OSV 6.24.020 was also exposed, which contains known critical CVEs).

Impact:

Downgrade Attacks: An attacker could downgrade the device to fw_7.08.002 or fw_7.08.025, reintroducing known vulnerabilities that were patched in the current 7.10.016 version, effectively bypassing all recent security updates.

Denial of Service (DoS): If an Info-User is allowed to trigger a firmware rollback or an unsupported update sequence, it could corrupt the firmware image, "brick" the device, and cause a permanent outage to the network's time synchronization service.

Remediation:

Enforce Backend Authorization: Immediately implement strict server-side checks to ensure that only Super-User accounts can access the Firmware Management page and trigger updates or schedules.

Remove Old Firmware: Delete outdated and vulnerable firmware files (fw_7.08.002, fw_7.08.025, and the OSV) from the device's storage to prevent accidental or malicious downgrades.

Code Signing: Implement cryptographic code signing to ensure the device only accepts trusted, up-to-date firmware images.

CVSS v4.0 Score: 7.1 (High)
CVSS v4.0 Vector: CVSS:4.0/AV:N/AC:L/AT:P/PR:L/UI:N/VC:N/VI:H/VA:H/SC:N/SI:N/SA:N
(Metric note: VI:H (High Integrity impact) because the attacker can alter the core firmware of the device. VA:H (High Availability impact) because a failed downgrade or bricking will take the time server offline).
